---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Updating Hew on Linux"
  description="How a new version of Hew reaches you on Linux, by how you installed it: AppImage, .deb or .rpm, Flatpak, Arch, or Gentoo."
>
  <div class="container help-page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Updating Hew on Linux</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Hew checks for a new version when it starts, and whenever you choose
        Check for Updates from the menu. What happens next depends on how you
        installed it. Find yours below.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Kinds of install">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#appimage">AppImage</a>: Hew updates itself.</li>
        <li><a href="#deb-rpm">.deb or .rpm</a>: Hew installs the update for you.</li>
        <li><a href="#flatpak">Flatpak</a>: Hew shows the command to run.</li>
        <li><a href="#arch">Arch Linux</a>: Hew shows the command to run.</li>
        <li><a href="#gentoo">Gentoo</a>: Hew shows the command to run.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Not sure which you have? If you downloaded a single file and ran it,
        it is the AppImage. If you installed it with a command or from a
        software store, it is one of the others.
      </p>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="appimage">AppImage</h2>
      <p>
        When a new version is out, Hew asks whether to install it. Choose
        <strong>Install</strong> and Hew replaces the AppImage file it is
        running from, then offers to restart. The file has to be somewhere
        you can write to, such as your home folder.
      </p>
      <p>
        If an update fails, download the new AppImage from the
        <a href="/download/">Download page</a> and put it where the old one
        was.
      </p>

      <h2 id="deb-rpm">.deb and .rpm</h2>
      <p>
        When a new version is out, Hew asks whether to install it. Choose
        <strong>Install</strong> and your computer asks for your password,
        because installing a package changes system files. Hew then offers to
        restart.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Did the update fail?</strong> Hew 1.4.0 and older could
        stop with "invalid updater binary format" on these installs. If an
        update from inside Hew doesn't work, download the new
        <code>.deb</code> or <code>.rpm</code> from the
        <a href="/download/">Download page</a> once and install it by
        double-clicking it or with one of these commands:
      </p>
      <pre><code>sudo apt install ./Hew_VERSION_amd64.deb
sudo dnf install ./Hew-VERSION-1.x86_64.rpm</code></pre>
      <p>
        Use the file name you downloaded, and the first command on Debian and
        Ubuntu, the second on Fedora. After that one manual update, updates
        work from inside Hew.
      </p>

      <h2 id="flatpak">Flatpak</h2>
      <p>
        Flatpak updates Hew, not Hew itself. When a new version is out, Hew
        shows the command and a <strong>Copy Command</strong> button. Paste
        it into a terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>flatpak update com.hew3d.Hew</code></pre>
      <p>
        A software store that supports Flatpak, such as GNOME Software or
        Discover, will also offer the update.
      </p>

      <h3>"Nothing to do"</h3>
      <p>
        If <code>flatpak update</code> says "Nothing to do" even though a new
        version is out, your copy was installed from a single-file bundle
        made before Hew 1.4, which didn't know where to look for updates. Add
        Hew's repository and reinstall once:
      </p>
      <pre><code>flatpak remote-add --user --if-not-exists hew https://hew3d.github.io/hew-packages/flatpak/com.hew3d.Hew.flatpakrepo
flatpak install --user --reinstall hew com.hew3d.Hew</code></pre>
      <p>
        If you installed Hew for everyone on the computer, leave out
        <code>--user</code> in both commands. Your settings and files are
        kept. From then on, <code>flatpak update</code> works.
      </p>

      <h2 id="arch">Arch Linux</h2>
      <p>
        Pacman updates Hew, not Hew itself. When a new version is out, Hew
        shows the command and a <strong>Copy Command</strong> button. Paste
        it into a terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>sudo pacman -Syu</code></pre>
      <p>
        Arch doesn't support updating a single package on its own, so this
        updates your whole system, Hew included.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gentoo">Gentoo</h2>
      <p>
        Portage updates Hew, not Hew itself. When a new version is out, Hew
        shows the command and a <strong>Copy Command</strong> button. Paste
        it into a terminal:
      </p>
      <pre><code>sudo emaint sync -r hew3d &amp;&amp; sudo emerge --update media-gfx/hew-bin</code></pre>

      <h2 id="other">Something else</h2>
      <p>
        If Hew came from another package manager, update it the same way you
        installed it. The <a href="/download/">Download page</a> always has
        the newest version, and the
        <a href="https://hew3d.github.io/hew-packages/">Arch, Gentoo, and
        Flatpak packages page</a> has the install steps for those.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .help-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  .page-head {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .page-head .intro {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .jump {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .jump ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    /* Keep a heading clear of the top edge when reached by anchor. */
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }

  .prose h3 {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
